  m_Name: TwoLobePumpData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: Two Lobe Pump
  engineDescription: A Two-Lobe Pump is a positive-displacement rotary pump used
    for transferring liquids and other fluids by trapping and moving fluid between
    two rotating lobed rotors and the pump casing. Its main components include the
    casing, two-lobed rotors, drive shaft, driven shaft, timing gears, inlet port,
    outlet port, bearings, and mechanical seals. During operation, the two rotors
    rotate in opposite directions and maintain a controlled clearance between their
    surfaces. As the lobes move away from the inlet region, they create an increasing
    volume that draws fluid into the pump. The trapped fluid is carried around the
    casing by the rotating lobes and transported toward the discharge side. As the
    lobes approach the outlet, the fluid is displaced from the pumping chamber and
    discharged under pressure. Two-lobe pumps are widely used in wastewater treatment,
    food and beverage processing, chemical industries, pharmaceutical applications,
    and industrial fluid transfer. From an engineering perspective, understanding
    rotor geometry, displacement, flow rate, pressure, volumetric efficiency, slip,
    sealing, pulsation, lubrication, wear, and maintenance is essential for achieving
    reliable and efficient pumping performance.
  engineCategory: Engineering
